    (
        4,
        24,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;the Lord thy God is a consuming fire, even a jealous God&#x27; (Deu 4:24), Ephrem reads the doubled-image (consuming fire + jealous God) as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of Hebrews 12:29 (&#x27;for our God is a consuming fire&#x27; — verbatim citation). The fire-image is not destructive wrath but the purifying jealousy that refuses rival affections. Tewahedo theology cites Deu 4:24 + Heb 12:29 as the canonical doubled-witness that the encounter-with-God is always simultaneously consoling and consuming; the Eucharist itself receives the same description in the Tewahedo Anaphora of Athanasius: &#x27;this is the burning coal which Isaiah saw&#x27;.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 4:23-31. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Heb 12:29 (verbatim citation).",
    ),
