    (
        19,
        15,
        "",
        "two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The two-witness rule.</strong> A capital case requires more than one witness. The principle is so foundational it shapes both later Jewish jurisprudence (Mishnah <em>Sanhedrin</em>) and Christian church discipline (Matt 18:16, 2 Cor 13:1, 1 Tim 5:19). Behind it: the Israelite court has no police, no forensic apparatus — testimony is the entire evidentiary basis, so the cost of false witness is high enough that 19:18–19 prescribes <em>lex talionis</em> against witnesses themselves who lie.",
        "User paraphrase; references Mishnah",
    ),
