    (
        30,
        6,
        "h",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;the Lord thy God will circumcise thine heart, and the heart of thy seed, to love the Lord thy God with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, that thou mayest live&#x27; (Deu 30:6), Ephrem reads the heart-circumcision-by-divine-action as the canonical Old-Testament promise of grace fulfilled in the new covenant: what Deu 10:16 commands (&#x27;circumcise your heart&#x27;), Deu 30:6 promises the Lord himself will accomplish. The same gospel-of-grace structure (command becomes promise) flows through Jer 31:33 and Ez 36:26 into Acts 2&#x27;s Pentecost. Tewahedo theosis-theology cites Deu 30:6 as the canonical Old-Testament anchor: the Spirit-given heart-circumcision IS theosis — God&#x27;s own action transforming the human heart to love him with the love that he commands.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 30:1-10. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Jer 31:33, Ez 36:26, Acts 2.",
    ),
