    (
        31,
        7,
        "",
        "Be strong and courageous",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Chazak v’ematz (<em>חֲזַק</em> <em>וֶאֱמָץ</em>).</strong> “Be strong and courageous.” Moses’ commissioning of Joshua, repeated to Joshua directly in Josh 1:6, 1:9. The phrase becomes the standard formula for transferring leadership in Israelite tradition; David uses it to commission Solomon (1 Chr 22:13, 28:20). It survives in modern Hebrew as a customary blessing — what one says at the conclusion of a stretch of public Torah-reading in the synagogue, marking the completion of one section and the courage required for the next.",
        "User original",
    ),
