    (
        33,
        9,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On Moses&#x27; blessing of Levi — &#x27;Who said unto his father and to his mother, I have not seen him; neither did he acknowledge his brethren, nor knew his own children: for they have observed thy word, and kept thy covenant&#x27; (Deu 33:9) — Ephrem reads the praise of Levi&#x27;s denial-of-kin-for-sake-of-God as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of the dominical &#x27;He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me&#x27; (Mt 10:37) and the Levitical-monastic ideal of leaving-family-for-God. Tewahedo monastic theology cites Deu 33:9 + Mt 10:37 as the canonical doubled-witness for the bahǝtawi&#x27;s renunciation of family ties — the desert father is a Levite in the pattern of Deu 33:9, whose covenant-keeping is honoured precisely because it preferred God to kin.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 33:8-11. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Mt 10:37.",
    ),
