    # ---- ch 34 ----
    (
        34,
        5,
        "",
        "Moses the servant of Yahweh died",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The death of Moses.</strong> The narrator’s problem is sharp: how does a book that Moses is traditionally said to have written record his own death? The Talmud (<em>Bava Batra</em> 14b–15a) preserves both options — that Joshua wrote the last eight verses, or that Moses wrote them himself in tears, prophetically. The text’s simple cadence (“and Moses the servant of Yahweh died there… and he was buried in the valley… and no one knows his grave to this day”) is one of the most haunting epitaphs in scripture. Jude 9 records a tradition that the archangel Michael disputed with Satan over the body of Moses — a midrashic expansion preserved in the lost work known as the Assumption of Moses.",
        "User paraphrase; references Talmud",
    ),
